Bollard-Jack
Ingredients: PUNK ROCK + NEW JACK SWING / SWINGBEAT + EUPHORIC, ENERGETIC & JOLLY + Choir
Creative Catalyst: In 2018, crate-diggers unsealed a 1991 municipal time capsule in Portsmouth, recovering a badly melted ferric cassette whose print-through bled a youth-club choir rehearsal directly over a DIY hardcore demo and an SP-1200 swingbeat session.

Bollard-Jack fuses the breakneck velocity and overdrive of late-70s street punk with the bouncy, heavily syncopated 16th-note swing of New Jack Swing, hoisting the entire ruckus into the heavens with soaring, room-shaking gospel choir arrangements. It sounds like an illegal dockside block party where rusted Telecasters duel with rattling E-mu drum machines, while a dozen powerhouse voices turn municipal frustration into pure, unbridled communal ecstasy.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 112–122 BPM; infectious swing quantize (typically 58% to 62% shuffle) grafted onto driving, double-time punk kick-and-snare patterns, creating a stomping, head-bobbing swagger.
- Harmonic Palette: Jubilant gospel turnaround cadences (IV–I and bVII–I progressions) colliding with abrasive, open-string punk power chords and bright, brassy vintage digital synth stabs.
- Production Signatures: (1) "Gated Slipway Snare" — massive, gated-reverb 12-bit SP-1200 snare hits blended beneath thrashing single-coil guitar slashes; (2) "Tape-Print Flutter" — deliberate analog pitch-wobble and magnetic print-through artifacts emulating the corroded time-capsule tape; (3) "Overdriven Pews" — 10-to-15-voice choral stacks recorded in cavernous dry docks and pushed through vintage tweed guitar amplifiers for crunchy, euphoric presence.
- Dynamics: Songs launch with unpolished dockyard count-ins or rhythmic clatter, erupt instantly into swaggering swingbeat verses, drop out to a sparse rhythm-and-handclap choir breakdown, then explode into a deafening, full-throttle chorale shout-along.

Scene & Origin Lore
Bollard-Jack sparked to life in the decommissioned naval dry docks and draughty scout huts of Portsmouth, UK, after local art-school students reverse-engineered the chaotic, heat-fused magnetic tape dug up during a waterfront redevelopment scheme. Believing the warbled mishmash of church harmonies, swingbeat drum breaks, and buzzsaw punk to be an intentional lost masterpiece from 1991, teenagers and off-duty ferry crews formed ad-hoc bands in drafty warehouses to recreate it live. The resulting subculture—dubbed "The Rigging"—dresses in high-visibility dockworker jackets, vintage 90s snapbacks, and salt-stained brothel creepers. Gigs operate like secular revival meetings: fans link arms in synchronized two-step bounces before colliding into good-natured, smiling circle pits, singing three-part choir harmonies at top volume over battered drum machines.

Central Instruments: Fender Telecaster, Rickenbacker 4003 bass guitar, E-mu SP-1200 sampler, Akai MPC60, Roland D-50 synthesizer (custom brass and mallet patches), 12-voice choir, metal dockyard chimes, heavy timber handclaps.

For Fans Of: The Clash (circa Sandinista!), Guy, Big Audio Dynamite, The Polyphonic Spree.

