Track 2: Maeve of the Lower Slipway
- Story & Context: In the damp tea canteen of the Trafalgar Boatyard, former choir directress Maeve Galloway finally confessed that it was her own hands that sealed the 1991 cassette inside the lead capsule, having secretly recorded her church ensemble over her younger brother’s illicit punk-rock rehearsal tape to rescue it from the bin. Her confession smelled faintly of diesel exhaust and peppermint tea as she admitted she deliberately let the magnetic tape oxidize to blur the lines between sacred jubilance and pub-cellar defiance. Strumming a nicked Fender Telecaster through a squealing transistor amp, she belted the track's vulnerable refrain: "I stole the parish high note and drove it through the floor."
